<article class="env-lemma" id="00EW">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00EW">Lemma <span data-tag="00EW">10.25.2</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a reduced ring. Then </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$R$ is a subring of a product of fields, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$R \to \prod _{\mathfrak p\text{ minimal}} R_{\mathfrak p}$ is an embedding into a product of fields, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\bigcup _{\mathfrak p\text{ minimal}} \mathfrak p$ is the set of zerodivisors of $R$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemma <a href="/tag/00EU" data-tag="00EU">10.25.1</a> each of the rings $R_\mathfrak p$ is a field. In particular, the kernel of the ring map $R \to R_\mathfrak p$ is $\mathfrak p$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/00E0" data-tag="00E0">10.17.2</a> we have $\bigcap _{\mathfrak p} \mathfrak p = (0)$. Hence (2) and (1) are true. If $x y = 0$ and $y \not= 0$, then $y \not\in \mathfrak p$ for some minimal prime $\mathfrak p$. Hence $x \in \mathfrak p$. Thus every zerodivisor of $R$ is contained in $\bigcup _{\mathfrak p\text{ minimal}} \mathfrak p$. Conversely, suppose that $x \in \mathfrak p$ for some minimal prime $\mathfrak p$. Then $x$ maps to zero in $R_\mathfrak p$, hence there exists $y \in R$, $y \not\in \mathfrak p$ such that $xy = 0$. In other words, $x$ is a zerodivisor. This finishes the proof of (3) and the lemma. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>